1. Does it need an active server/database?

No. For your fake-site lab, it does not need an active website/server unless you want centralized collection from multiple browsers/machines.

For a local/manual workflow, better options are:

Best simple option: Tampermonkey local storage + export button

The script can keep scraped posts in Tampermonkey storage using:

GM_setValue('scrapedPosts', postsArray)
GM_getValue('scrapedPosts', [])

Then you add an Export JSON or Export CSV button to download the accumulated log manually.

This is probably the best fit for:

one browser,
fake websites,
periodic manual review,
no backend setup,
no API/server required.
Better medium option: download a JSON/CSV log periodically

The script already has an exportData() function, but it only exports the current in-memory scrapedData array. That means once the page reloads, some unsynced data could disappear.

A better version would:

append every match to persistent Tampermonkey storage,
dedupe by platform + id,
allow manual export,
optionally clear the log after export.

Conceptually:

function savePostsLocally(newPosts) {
    const existing = GM_getValue('scrapedPosts', []);
    const seen = new Set(existing.map(p => `${p.platform}:${p.id}`));

    for (const post of newPosts) {
        const key = `${post.platform}:${post.id}`;
        if (!seen.has(key)) {
            existing.push(post);
            seen.add(key);
        }
    }

    GM_setValue('scrapedPosts', existing);
}

Then the scraper becomes more like a local evidence/log collector instead of a server-syncing scraper.

When a server/database is useful

You only need an active server if you want:

multiple users/browsers writing to one shared place,
dashboards,
remote access,
automatic backups,
long-term storage beyond one browser profile,
real-time monitoring.

For your stated use case, I would remove the database sync entirely and replace it with a local persistent log plus manual export.

