3. How to update selectors for modern social-media-style architecture

For a fake training environment, I would not model the scraper around old static CSS classes like:

.tweet
.username
.userContentWrapper
.fbUserStory

Modern social media sites tend to use:

React/Vue/Angular-rendered components,
frequently changing generated class names,
semantic roles like role="article",
accessibility labels,
test IDs,
embedded JSON state,
infinite scroll feeds,
lazy-loaded content,
route changes without full page reloads.

For your fake websites, you should intentionally design the fake DOM to resemble that architecture.

Better selector strategy

Use a priority order like this:

Stable training-specific data attributes
ARIA/role-based selectors
Structural fallback selectors
Text/content heuristics
Embedded state extraction, if your fake app includes hydration data

Example:

const POST_SELECTORS = [
    '[data-testid="post"]',
    '[data-post-id]',
    '[role="article"]',
    'article',
    '[aria-label*="Post"]'
];

For post text:

const TEXT_SELECTORS = [
    '[data-testid="post-text"]',
    '[data-testid="tweetText"]',
    '[data-testid="caption"]',
    '[aria-label="Post text"]',
    '[lang]',
    '.post-content'
];

For username:

const USER_SELECTORS = [
    '[data-testid="user-name"]',
    '[data-testid="User-Name"]',
    '[data-author]',
    'a[href^="/@"]',
    'a[href*="/profile/"]',
    '[aria-label*="profile"]'
];

For timestamp:

const TIME_SELECTORS = [
    'time[datetime]',
    '[data-testid="timestamp"]',
    '[data-time]',
    'abbr[data-utime]'
];
Better extraction helper

Instead of hardcoding every platform separately, create generic helpers:

function firstMatch(root, selectors) {
    for (const selector of selectors) {
        const el = root.querySelector(selector);
        if (el) return el;
    }
    return null;
}

function textFrom(root, selectors) {
    const el = firstMatch(root, selectors);
    return el ? el.innerText.trim() : '';
}

function attrFrom(root, selectors, attr) {
    const el = firstMatch(root, selectors);
    return el ? el.getAttribute(attr) : null;
}

Then your fake-platform scraper becomes easier to maintain:

function scrapeGenericFeed(platformConfig) {
    const posts = [];

    const postElements = findAllBySelectors(platformConfig.postSelectors)
        .slice(0, CONFIG.maxPostsPerCheck);

    for (const el of postElements) {
        const text = textFrom(el, platformConfig.textSelectors);
        const user = textFrom(el, platformConfig.userSelectors);
        const timeEl = firstMatch(el, platformConfig.timeSelectors);

        if (!text || !user) continue;

        posts.push({
            id: getPostId(el, platformConfig),
            text,
            user,
            time: parsePostTime(timeEl),
            location: textFrom(el, platformConfig.locationSelectors) || extractLocationFromText(text),
            platform: CONFIG.platform,
            url: getPostUrl(el, platformConfig),
            timestamp: Date.now()
        });
    }

    return posts;
}

Then each fake site gets a config:

const PLATFORM_CONFIGS = {
    x: {
        postSelectors: ['[data-testid="tweet"]', '[role="article"]', 'article'],
        textSelectors: ['[data-testid="tweetText"]', '[lang]', '.post-content'],
        userSelectors: ['[data-testid="User-Name"]', '[data-author]', 'a[href^="/@"]'],
        timeSelectors: ['time[datetime]', '[data-time]'],
        locationSelectors: ['[data-testid="place"]', '[data-location]', '.location'],
        postUrlPattern: '/status/'
    },

    instagram: {
        postSelectors: ['article', '[data-testid="post"]', '[role="article"]'],
        textSelectors: ['[data-testid="post-caption"]', '[aria-label="Caption"]', '.caption'],
        userSelectors: ['[data-testid="post-username"]', 'a[href^="/"]'],
        timeSelectors: ['time[datetime]', '[data-testid="timestamp"]'],
        locationSelectors: ['[data-testid="location-link"]', '[data-location]', '.location'],
        postUrlPattern: '/p/'
    },

    facebook: {
        postSelectors: ['[role="article"]', '[data-testid="post"]', 'article'],
        textSelectors: ['[data-testid="post_message"]', '[data-ad-preview="message"]', '.post-content'],
        userSelectors: ['[data-testid="story-subtitle"]', '[data-author]', 'strong a'],
        timeSelectors: ['time[datetime]', 'abbr[data-utime]', '[data-time]'],
        locationSelectors: ['[data-location]', '.location'],
        postUrlPattern: '/posts/'
    }
};

That is closer to modern architecture without depending on one brittle class name.

4. Is there risk storing fake credentials?

Yes, but the risk is much lower if they are truly fake and isolated.

The main risks are:

Risk	Why it matters
Credential reuse	Someone may accidentally use a real password pattern
Browser/profile compromise	Tampermonkey storage can be read by scripts/extensions with enough access
Bad habits	Storing plaintext passwords normalizes a pattern you would not want in real tools
Cross-environment leakage	Fake credentials might accidentally get copied into logs, exports, screenshots, or repos

For a closed lab, acceptable precautions would be:

use fake-only accounts,
use random fake passwords,
never reuse real passwords,
do not commit credentials into GitHub,
allow a “clear credentials” button,
label the UI clearly as “training credentials only.”

A better fake-lab design is to store account tokens like:

{
  username: "bot_001",
  token: "lab-token-001",
  platform: "x"
}

instead of pretending to store real passwords. That keeps the lesson focused on credential rotation without encouraging bad password handling.

5. Would running once an hour make it less fragile?

Yes, in several ways.

Running every 5 seconds is aggressive:

checkInterval: 5000

For your training setup, hourly or semi-hourly runs would be better unless the exercise is specifically about high-frequency scraping detection.

Benefits of running less often:

fewer DOM reads,
fewer duplicate records,
less browser load,
fewer false positives from partially loaded feeds,
fewer fake-site security triggers,
easier debugging,
cleaner logs.

I would change the interval to something like:

checkInterval: 60 * 60 * 1000 // 1 hour

or for testing:

checkInterval: 5 * 60 * 1000 // 5 minutes

For a fake security lab, a useful configuration would be:

const CONFIG = {
    mode: GM_getValue('mode', 'training'),
    checkInterval: GM_getValue('checkInterval', 60 * 60 * 1000),
    maxPostsPerCheck: GM_getValue('maxPostsPerCheck', 25)
};

Then you can quickly switch between:

Mode	Interval	Purpose
Debug	30 seconds	Test selectors
Training	5–15 minutes	Observe defenses
Normal	1 hour	Simulate low-frequency collection

One note: lower frequency makes the script less noisy, but it does not fix bad selectors, SPA timing issues, or bad timestamp parsing. Those need separate fixes.

